<%# The shape every admin record page has: one column, one header, one section stack.

    **Declared here rather than nine times.** The three school pages were measured before this existed and
    disagreed on all of it: view was 768px with three cards, edit 768px with two, create 1062px with one and no
    section headings at all. A form's measure should not change between creating a record and editing it -
    design.md records that defect on `classrooms#edit` - and a section that looks different on each page is the
    inconsistency this partial exists to remove.

    **View and edit are the same page.** Stripe's customer page, Linear's project page, Shopify admin's product
    page and Polaris's resource detail pages all hold a record's attributes *and* its collections on one
    screen, edited in place. A separate edit screen is a Rails scaffold convention; it earns its place for a
    long form, not for a record with a handful of fields.

    Locals:
      title       - the record's own name, which is the page's h1
      breadcrumbs - the trail
      description - optional line under the title
      badge       - optional status pill, rendered on the title's line
      actions     - optional block for the header's actions, usually just Delete

    `badge` is a pass-through to `_page_header`, which has had the slot all along - a status is not an
    action and does not belong in the actions block. Three record pages were putting their state in the
    *description* instead, joined to the metadata by the same separator, so "trading on" read as a third
    attribute after the grade and the year.

    The sections themselves are the block, and **the order is per page, not per shape.** Eight of the nine
    record pages lead with Details, because editing the record is why you opened it. `schools#show` leads with
    its years, because provisioning them is - measured, with Details above them the years started at y=618 of
    a 625px viewport. So the question is what this page is for, and the answer is not the same on all nine.

    This paragraph read "a collection first where there is one, because that is what the page is for" - which
    was the school page's reason generalised into a rule, stated on the partial that renders the eight pages
    that do the opposite. A docstring that contradicts every caller is worse than none. %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
  <%= admin_breadcrumbs(breadcrumbs) %>

  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: title, description: local_assigns[:description],
                       badge: local_assigns[:badge] } do %>
    <%= local_assigns[:actions] %>
  <% end %>

  <div class="space-y-6"><%= yield %></div>
</div>
